From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 08:20:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 188/190] Sega CD: hand the servant every screen-to-screen
 blit, on a tile grid

Two halves of the same thing, for window scrolling.

bb_screen() is the AES's screen-to-screen blit, and the desktop scrolls
a window with it: blit the part that stays, redraw only the strip that is
new (deskwin.c win_blt). Measured on a C: window with the arrow key held,
379 tiles converted per scroll step -- about eight frames of the
servant's pump, and the pixels that merely moved are most of it. The blit
is now published where the servant can see it, so it can move them by
permuting its tiles instead of converting them.

That only works if the distance is a whole number of eight-line tile
rows, and it never was: the desktop's row pitch is the item height plus
its gap, 42 for icons and 10 for text, so every scroll landed two pixels
off the grid. win_view() now rounds the pitch down to a multiple of
eight -- 40 and 8 -- which takes the gap and nothing else, both still at
least as tall as the thing they space.

With both, the servant's ring engages and the rows that moved cost
nothing: inside it, exactly the newly exposed strip converts, 164 tiles
for the five tile rows a scroll step exposes.

What it does not yet pay for is the edges. A window's work area is its
outer rectangle plus (1, 21) -- see wm_calc -- so it never lands on the
tile grid, and the two partial columns and two partial rows around it
repaint every scroll: another 157 tiles. Net is about 15%, which is
inside the run-to-run noise. Finishing it means landing work areas on a
16x8 grid, which is a change to where windows are allowed to sit.

---
 aes/gemgraf.c  |  6 ++++++
 bios/segacd.c  | 39 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 bios/segacd.h  |  4 ++++
 desk/deskwin.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 70 insertions(+)

diff --git a/aes/gemgraf.c b/aes/gemgraf.c
index 3e9e741..5a6ff6c 100644
--- a/aes/gemgraf.c
+++ b/aes/gemgraf.c
@@ -28,6 +28,9 @@
 #include "gemdos.h"
 
 #include "gemgraf.h"
+#ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
+#include "../bios/segacd.h"
+#endif
 #include "gemgsxif.h"
 #include "optimopt.h"
 #include "gsx2.h"
@@ -360,6 +363,9 @@ void bb_screen(WORD scsx, WORD scsy, WORD scdx, WORD scdy,
     gsx_blt(NULL, scsx, scsy,
             scdx, scdy,
             scw, sch, S_ONLY, -1, -1);
+#ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
+    segacd_blitted(scsx, scsy, scdx, scdy, scw, sch);
+#endif
 }
 
 
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index fff7f73..dc29e98 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -4235,6 +4235,45 @@ void segacd_scrolled(WORD rows)
     sb[2] = (UWORD)(sb[2] + rows);
 }
 
+/* ...and the other thing that moves a lot of screen at once: bb_screen(),
+ * the AES's screen-to-screen blit. The desktop scrolls a window with it
+ * (deskwin.c win_blt: blit the part that stays, redraw only the strip
+ * that is new), and so do the file selector and the window manager.
+ *
+ * Measured on a C: window of eleven items, the arrow key held: 310 tiles
+ * converted per scroll step, about seven frames of the servant's pump.
+ * The pixels that moved are the whole of that; the strip that is
+ * genuinely new is a fraction of it.
+ *
+ * The servant can move them without converting anything -- the same ring
+ * as the console scroll, scoped to a rectangle instead of the screen --
+ * so hand it the blit verbatim and let it work out what it can use.
+ * Published after the blit, so a sequence number that has moved is a
+ * screen that has finished moving.
+ *
+ * Every blit, not only the desktop's: this says what happened, and what
+ * to make of it is the servant's business. It ignores the ones it cannot
+ * use, which is most of them. */
+#define BLTBLK_OFF  32216UL     /* past the scroll block at 32208 */
+
+void segacd_blitted(WORD sx, WORD sy, WORD dx, WORD dy, WORD w, WORD h)
+{
+    volatile UWORD *bb = (volatile UWORD *)(v_bas_ad + BLTBLK_OFF);
+
+    if (v_lin_wr != 160)
+        return;                 /* not the screen the servant maps */
+    bb[3] = (UWORD)sx;
+    bb[4] = (UWORD)sy;
+    bb[5] = (UWORD)dx;
+    bb[6] = (UWORD)dy;
+    bb[7] = (UWORD)w;
+    bb[8] = (UWORD)h;
+    bb[0] = 0x424C;             /* "BL" */
+    bb[1] = 0x4954;             /* "IT" */
+    bb[2] = (UWORD)(bb[2] + 1); /* last, so the fields are there first */
+}
+
+
 /******************************************************************************/
 /* System timer                                                               */
 /******************************************************************************/
diff --git a/bios/segacd.h b/bios/segacd.h
index bbf5c78..c35924e 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.h
+++ b/bios/segacd.h
@@ -133,6 +133,10 @@ void segacd_buram_call(struct brmcall *c);      /* segacd2.S */
  * repainting the screen. Signed: scroll_down goes the other way. */
 void segacd_scrolled(WORD rows);
 
+/* Every screen-to-screen blit, verbatim. The servant turns the ones it
+ * can into a rotation and ignores the rest. */
+void segacd_blitted(WORD sx, WORD sy, WORD dx, WORD dy, WORD w, WORD h);
+
 
 #endif /* MACHINE_SEGACD */
 
diff --git a/desk/deskwin.c b/desk/deskwin.c
index efd7d10..b63568e 100644
--- a/desk/deskwin.c
+++ b/desk/deskwin.c
@@ -88,6 +88,27 @@ void win_view(void)
     }
     G.g_iwspc = G.g_iwext + G.g_iwint;
     G.g_ihspc = G.g_ihext + G.g_ihint;
+#ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
+    /* Snap the row pitch to the servant's tile grid.
+     *
+     * A window is scrolled by blitting the part that stays and
+     * redrawing the strip that is new, and the servant can move the
+     * blitted part without converting a pixel of it -- but only if the
+     * distance is a whole number of eight-line tile rows, because a
+     * tile is the unit it holds the screen in. The pitch decides that
+     * distance: it is 42 for icons and 10 for text, so every scroll
+     * step landed two pixels off the grid and every one of them was
+     * repainted in full. 310 tiles a step, measured on a C: window.
+     *
+     * Down rather than up, and the whitespace is all that goes: 42
+     * becomes 40 and 10 becomes 8, both still at least the height of
+     * the thing they space, so nothing is clipped and nothing
+     * overlaps. Two pixels of gap for a scroll that costs a fraction
+     * of what it did. */
+    G.g_ihspc &= ~7;
+    if (G.g_ihspc < G.g_ihext)
+        G.g_ihspc = (G.g_ihext + 7) & ~7;
+#endif
 #if CONF_WITH_SIZE_TO_FIT
     {
     GRECT t;
